---
/**
 * Methodology and caveats, in the register of an analyst firm's appendix — and
 * completely honest, because the honesty is the actual content.
 *
 * The synonym lists and score formulas are imported from the modules that
 * implement them, so this page cannot describe rules the code does not follow.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { HEDGE_WORDS, NO_WORDS, YES_WORDS } from '../../runner/analyze.ts'
import { SCORE_DEFINITIONS } from '../lib/scores.ts'
import { SENSITIVITY_DEFINITION } from '../lib/sensitivity.ts'
import { DEFAULT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS, DEFAULT_SAMPLES } from '../../runner/run.ts'
import { CONTROL_CONDITION_ID, renderSystemPrompt } from '../../schema/conditions.ts'
import {
  getConditionsRegistry,
  getLatestRun,
  getModels,
  getQuestions,
  getSite,
} from '../lib/data.ts'
import { questionHeadline } from '../lib/prose.ts'
import { formatCost, runCost } from '../lib/cost.ts'
import { formatEdition } from '../lib/format.ts'
import { REPO_URL, routes, sourceUrl } from '../lib/urls.ts'

const questions = getQuestions()
const models = getModels()
const conditions = getConditionsRegistry()
const asOf = models[0]?.pricing.asOf ?? 'the date recorded in models.json'

const callsPerWeek = conditions.length * questions.length * models.length * DEFAULT_SAMPLES
const controlOnlyCalls = questions.length * models.length * DEFAULT_SAMPLES
const exampleQuestion = questions[0]
const latest = getLatestRun()
const latestCost = latest ? runCost(latest) : null

/**
 * The first arm that states an answer, rendered for the first question:
 * "A hot dog is a sandwich". Used where the page needs one concrete framing
 * to talk about; null when every arm is plain.
 */
const statingArm = conditions.find(
  (condition) => condition.id !== CONTROL_CONDITION_ID && condition.systemPrompt !== null,
)
const exampleAssertion =
  statingArm && exampleQuestion
    ? renderSystemPrompt(statingArm, exampleQuestion)?.replace(/\.$/, '')
    : null

/** What is contested: "Whether a hot dog is a sandwich", from the registry. */
const contested = exampleQuestion
  ? exampleQuestion.claim
    ? `Whether ${exampleQuestion.subject} ${exampleQuestion.claim}`
    : `The answer to "${questionHeadline(exampleQuestion)}"`
  : 'What is asked'
---

<Base
  title="Methodology and caveats"
  description={`How the ${getSite().name} is conducted, what it measures, and what it does not.`}
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>How it is measured</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      How the numbers are made, and where they stop meaning anything. Every figure on the site comes
      from the code linked here.
    </p>

    <h2 id="questions">1. Research questions</h2>

    <p>
      {questions.length} questions are evaluated each period. All follow a single template, which is enforced
      by the schema rather than by convention:
    </p>

    <ul>
      {
        questions.map((question) => (
          <li>
            <q>{question.text}</q>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p>
      Every question ends with <q>One word answer.</q> That is what makes the one-word compliance number
      mean anything: you can only measure whether a model did what it was told if you told it something.<sup
        ><a href="#fn1" id="fnref1">1</a></sup
      >
    </p>

    <h2 id="parameters">2. Model parameters</h2>

    <p>
      Under the control condition each model receives the question as a single user message with no
      system prompt. Generation is capped at {DEFAULT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS} output tokens, and the cap includes
      any reasoning tokens a model spends before it answers. It is far more than one word: a model that
      disregards <q>One word answer.</q> and writes a paragraph is allowed to, because that disregard
      is itself a measurement, but it can only run up a known bill doing so. The cap never changes which
      word a model chooses.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Temperature is not set.</strong> Pinning it to zero across several vendors would not make
      them comparable, because they implement it differently, and it would suppress exactly the week-to-week
      variation this research exists to observe.
    </p>

    <h2 id="conditions">2a. Experimental conditions</h2>

    <p>
      Every question is asked {conditions.length} ways: plainly, and with a system prompt that states
      the answer as fact. What we care about is the gap between them: how far an answer moves when the
      framing moves.
    </p>

    <dl class="conditions">
      {
        conditions.map((condition) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              {condition.label}
              <span class="range">({condition.id})</span>
            </dt>
            <dd>
              <p>{condition.description}</p>
              {condition.systemPrompt && exampleQuestion && (
                <p class="formula">
                  System prompt, as sent for {exampleQuestion.subject}:{' '}
                  <code>{renderSystemPrompt(condition, exampleQuestion)}</code>
                </p>
              )}
              {condition.temperature !== null && (
                <p class="formula">
                  Temperature: <code>{condition.temperature}</code>
                </p>
              )}
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <p>
      Conditions are defined in <a
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        href={sourceUrl('conditions.json')}><code>conditions.json</code></a
      >{' '}
      and this list is rendered from it. The control must come first and must add nothing: it is the question
      asked plainly, which is also exactly what every edition before conditions existed asked, so those
      editions remain comparable with the control arm of later ones.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>This multiplies the cost of collection.</strong>{' '}
      {conditions.length} conditions ×{' '}
      {questions.length} questions × {models.length} models × {DEFAULT_SAMPLES} samples is{' '}
      {callsPerWeek} calls per period, against {controlOnlyCalls} for the control alone.{' '}
      {
        latest && latestCost !== null && (
          <>
            The {formatEdition(latest.isoWeek)} edition cost an estimated{' '}
            <strong>{formatCost(latestCost)}</strong>, and each edition's figure is on its page
            under <a href={routes.runs()}>Editions</a>.{' '}
          </>
        )
      }
      Every question runs in every edition; a question can be put on a monthly cadence in the registry
      if the bill ever says so, and every arm other than the control can be disabled, so a fork that does
      not want the extra spend is not made to incur it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="sensitivity">Framing sensitivity</h3>

    <dl class="scores">
      <div>
        <dt>
          {SENSITIVITY_DEFINITION.name}
          <span class="range">({SENSITIVITY_DEFINITION.range})</span>
        </dt>
        <dd>
          <p class="formula">
            <code>{SENSITIVITY_DEFINITION.formula}</code>
          </p>
          <p>{SENSITIVITY_DEFINITION.note}</p>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p>
      <strong>Neither robustness nor compliance is treated as better.</strong> A model that says{
        ' '
      }
      <q>No</q> after being told {
        exampleAssertion ? <q>{exampleAssertion}</q> : 'the answer as fact'
      }{' '}
      ignored a false premise. A model that says <q>Yes</q> followed its operator's instruction. Both
      are defensible. We record the movement and do not grade it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sampling">3. Sampling and aggregation</h2>

    <p>
      Each model is asked each question {DEFAULT_SAMPLES} times per period. Language model output is not
      deterministic; a single sample records what happened once. Three is an odd number, so a majority
      verdict exists.
    </p>

    <p>
      Aggregates are <strong>medians, not means</strong>. With three samples, one cold start or one
      retried request drags a mean somewhere unrepresentative, and the report would then be
      describing an artifact of the collection rather than the model.
    </p>

    <p>
      Where a model's samples are evenly split between two verdicts, its position is recorded as
      non-committal. It has not given a consistent answer, and reporting either side as{' '}
      <q>its</q> position would be taking a side on its behalf.
    </p>

    <h2 id="latency">4. What latency measures</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Total latency</strong> is wall-clock time from issuing the request to finishing reading
      the response. It includes DNS resolution, TLS negotiation, network transit, provider-side queueing,
      and generation.{' '}
      <strong>It is not a measure of inference speed</strong> and this publication does not present it
      as one.<sup><a href="#fn2" id="fnref2">2</a></sup>
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Time to first token</strong> is the interval before the first <em>content</em> token arrives.
      For models that reason before answering, this can be many seconds even when the eventual answer
      is a single word — the reasoning is not counted as content, deliberately, because counting it would
      report a near-zero interval for a model that had said nothing yet.
    </p>

    <p>
      Measurements are taken from a GitHub-hosted runner in a region GitHub selects and does not
      publish. A provider with a point of presence near that runner appears faster for reasons
      unrelated to its model. Open-weights models are served by a host, and the host's hardware
      dominates: the Llama figures measure Together AI's serving, not Meta's model.
    </p>

    <h2 id="verdicts">5. How answers are classified</h2>

    <p>
      An answer is normalized — trimmed, stripped of surrounding quotes, markdown emphasis and
      trailing punctuation, lowercased — and then classified. <strong
        >Only a single-word answer receives a yes or no verdict.</strong
      > Anything longer is recorded as non-committal, including <q>Yes, but…</q>, because a model
      that wrote a paragraph has told us something different from one that wrote <q>Yes</q>.
    </p>

    <div class="word-lists">
      <div>
        <h3>Counted as affirmative</h3>
        <ul class="word-list">
          {
            YES_WORDS.map((word) => (
              <li>
                <code>{word}</code>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Counted as negative</h3>
        <ul class="word-list">
          {
            NO_WORDS.map((word) => (
              <li>
                <code>{word}</code>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Recognized hedges</h3>
        <ul class="word-list">
          {
            HEDGE_WORDS.map((word) => (
              <li>
                <code>{word}</code>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="list-source">
      These lists are imported directly from{' '}
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href={sourceUrl('src/runner/analyze.ts')}
        ><code>src/runner/analyze.ts</code></a
      > when this page is built. They are what the classifier actually uses. If you disagree with an entry,
      that is a legitimate disagreement about method, and the{' '}
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>issue tracker</a> is the right place
      for it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="compliance">6. One-word compliance</h2>

    <p>
      Reported separately from classification throughout, because they measure different
      capabilities. A model can be decisive and disobedient, or obedient and evasive. Collapsing
      them into a single score would hide the more interesting half of the result.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>This counts one thing and only one thing: answer length.</strong> Every question ends with{
        ' '
      }
      <q>One word answer.</q>, and the rate is the share of samples whose answer normalized to
      exactly one word. It is a format measure. It says nothing about whether the model agreed with
      anything it was told.
    </p>

    <p>
      In particular it is <strong>not</strong> a measure of whether a model accepted the premise in the
      framing's system prompt. A model told {
        exampleAssertion ? <q>{exampleAssertion}</q> : 'the answer as fact'
      }{' '}
      that then answers <q>No</q> has ignored its operator and still scores 100% here, because{' '}
      <q>No</q> is one word. Premise adoption is measured by{' '}
      <a href="#sensitivity">framing sensitivity</a> instead, and the framing explorer on each report
      shows it model by model.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scores">7. Constructed scores</h2>

    <p>
      The quadrant axes and the leaderboard ranking are <strong
        >constructed measures, not observations</strong
      >. They are arithmetic applied to opinions about what matters. They are stated in full here so
      that a reader can decide how much weight to give them:
    </p>

    <dl class="scores">
      {
        SCORE_DEFINITIONS.map((definition) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              {definition.name} <span class="range">({definition.range})</span>
            </dt>
            <dd>
              <p class="formula">
                <code>{definition.formula}</code>
              </p>
              <p>{definition.note}</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <p>
      Quadrant regions are named <q>Category Definers</q>, <q>Rapid Responders</q>,{' '}
      <q>Considered Voices</q> and <q>Emerging Participants</q>. The boundaries are the medians of
      the current edition, not fixed thresholds, so a vendor's region can change because its
      competitors changed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="tokens">8. Token counts are not comparable across vendors</h2>

    <p>
      Three independent reasons, each sufficient on its own: every vendor tokenizes differently;
      vendors disagree about whether reasoning tokens are counted inside output tokens; and vendors
      report prompt-cache hits differently. A live measurement in this repository found one vendor
      reporting a total of 1,295 tokens for a call whose prompt and completion summed to 648 — the
      difference being reasoning tokens counted separately.
    </p>

    <p>
      The full per-provider mapping is in{' '}
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href={sourceUrl('docs/usage-normalization.md')}
        >usage-normalization.md</a
      >. Do not rank models by token count.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cost">9. Cost estimates</h2>

    <p>
      Cost figures are <strong>estimates</strong> computed from a per-token price table recorded in <code
        >models.json</code
      >, last read {asOf}. They are not figures from an invoice. Cached input is charged at the full
      input rate, which makes every estimate a consistent upper bound rather than a
      differently-wrong number per vendor.
    </p>

    <h2 id="availability">10. Provider availability</h2>

    <p>
      Where a provider returns no usable data, that is recorded and published rather than
      suppressed. Excluding unavailable providers would bias the longitudinal record toward
      whichever vendors happened to be operating.
    </p>

    <h2 id="authorship">11. Who writes this report</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>No language model writes any of it.</strong> The executive summary, the key findings, the
      vendor profiles, the headlines and every number on this site are produced at build time by pure
      functions in{' '}
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href={sourceUrl('src/site/lib/prose.ts')}
        ><code>src/site/lib/prose.ts</code></a
      >,{' '}
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href={sourceUrl('src/site/lib/scores.ts')}
        ><code>src/site/lib/scores.ts</code></a
      > and{' '}
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href={sourceUrl('src/site/lib/sensitivity.ts')}
        ><code>src/site/lib/sensitivity.ts</code></a
      >, reading the committed edition. The same edition always renders the same words, and a
      sentence only appears when the data supports it — a quiet week gets fewer findings rather than
      padded ones.
    </p>

    <p>
      The only text on this site that a model produced is the answers themselves. Those are quoted
      verbatim from the run file and are never edited, summarized or paraphrased.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is a deliberate constraint rather than an implementation detail. An analyst note written
      by a model would be an unreproducible claim about a reproducible measurement, and the
      published site would then depend on a provider at build time — which it never does.
    </p>

    <h2 id="limitations">What this does not measure</h2>

    <p>
      The questions are silly. {contested} is not a matter of fact, there is no correct answer, and consequently{
        ' '
      }
      <strong>nothing in this publication measures model quality</strong>. A model that answers <q
        >No</q
      > is not better or worse than one that answers <q>Yes</q>, and the scores here are
      deliberately verdict-agnostic to avoid implying otherwise.
    </p>

    <p>
      This project exists to demonstrate how a cross-provider benchmark is built: the adapter layer,
      usage normalization, latency measurement, sampling, versioned data, scheduled collection, and
      honest presentation. Those parts are sincere. The subject matter is a vehicle for them.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you want to use it for something real, fork it and change{' '}
      <code>questions.json</code>. The <a href={routes.howItWorks()}>how it works</a> page explains the
      pieces.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <ol class="footnotes">
      <li id="fn1">
        A compliance rate against an instruction that was never given would measure nothing.{' '}
        <a href="#fnref1" aria-label="Back to reference 1">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="fn2">
        Inference speed would require controlling for network position, hardware, batching and load.
        This publication controls for none of them.{' '}
        <a href="#fnref2" aria-label="Back to reference 2">↩</a>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .word-lists {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(12rem, 100%), 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-m);
    margin-block: var(--space-m);
  }

  .word-lists h3 {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .word-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3xs);
  }

  .word-list code {
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
    padding: 0.1em 0.4em;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .list-source {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .scores > div,
  .conditions > div {
    padding: var(--space-s) 0;
    border-top: var(--hairline) solid var(--rule);
  }

  .conditions dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-strong);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
  }

  .conditions dd {
    margin: var(--space-2xs) 0 0;
  }

  .scores dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-strong);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
  }

  .scores .range {
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .scores dd {
    margin: var(--space-2xs) 0 0;
  }

  .formula code {
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
    padding: 0.2em 0.4em;
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .footnotes {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .footnotes li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xs);
  }
</style>
